unpatched matches the software you run against public vulnerability data.
The code is MIT. The data keeps the terms of the people who publish it:

CVE Program (cvelistV5)
  CVE records come from the CVE Program, https://www.cve.org. CVE is a
  registered trademark of The MITRE Corporation. Use of the records is under
  the CVE Terms of Use: https://www.cve.org/Legal/TermsOfUse
  Records may include CISA ADP (Vulnrichment) data, published under CC0.

NVD
  This product uses data from the NVD API but is not endorsed or certified by
  the NVD. https://nvd.nist.gov

OSV
  Data from OSV.dev (https://osv.dev) is licensed under CC-BY 4.0:
  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ . Each record names its own
  upstream database; those databases may carry their own licenses.

CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog
  Published by the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency.
  Public domain (US government work). https://www.cisa.gov/kev

EPSS
  Exploit Prediction Scoring System, by FIRST (Forum of Incident Response and
  Security Teams). Used under FIRST's terms, with attribution:
  https://www.first.org/epss

endoflife.date
  End-of-life dates and release data from https://endoflife.date, MIT License.

Exploit-DB and Metasploit
  Only CVE identifiers from the Exploit-DB index (Offensive Security) and the
  Metasploit Framework module metadata (Rapid7) are used, to tell whether a
  public exploit exists. No exploit code or description is stored or
  redistributed.

The prebuilt database (unpatched-db.sqlite.zst) contains facts and identifiers
derived from the sources above. It carries no warranty: see "Not a guarantee"
in the guide. Third-party Rust crates keep their own licenses; `cargo deny
check licenses` checks them (see deny.toml).
